And sometimes you don’t know who you are until you see yourself at the bottom.

Rock bottom is both a mirror and a springboard.

The mirror strips away the illusions—who you thought you were, what you thought mattered, what you thought you needed.

But the bounce is interesting.

The same force that brought you down can become the force that propels you upward, provided you can redirect it.

And there’s a paradox: The bottom isn’t necessarily where you lose yourself. It might be where you finally recognize yourself.

You can shatter the mirror, stare into it forever, or use what it reveals to change your trajectory.

There’s another layer to your metaphor, too: a mirror reflects, but it also reverses your perspective.

Sometimes the way out isn’t to push harder in the same direction.

It’s to recognize that you’ve been looking at the world upside down.

The bottom can become a foundation rather than a destination.